<td align="left" width="20px"><b>Answer:</b></td>
<td align="left"><b>John</b> - 21/01/2004 20:39:59
</td></tr><tr><td>&nbsp;</td><td>Hi Scott,<br>
<br>
Well thanks for making that very easy to find you! I'm glad that you still have an interest in this field, im aware you're a busy man. It seems from Kidd's book that you were ahead of the game back then.<br>
<br>
I have done a few small tests on gyroscopes that have shown me they are worth persuing for propulsion. I hoped before I moved onto copying patents, Kidds or Laithwaites or your own, or all of them, to try a more simple proof of principle (im neither well off nor especially money motivated) . The Strachan Machine, using only one gyroscope seemed to offer this, but I am unable to find any information on it. I dont want to reinvent the wheel, and feel this technology should be moved on quickly at this point. What I would like to do is test the simpler Strachan Machine on a trolley, on water then in air (using helium balloons to counter the weight). At that point, on success, some team work would be needed to get a radio controlled version, a twin/quad gyro system etc. Save that patent going to waste.<br>
<br>
I dont know if you still have the machine on a trolley, or would be willing to share any information on it? Id be happy to e-mail you privately if you need to know more about me.<br>
<br>
Anyway, thanks for letting us know you're still about.<br>
<br>
John<br>
<br><br></td></tr>
